      "writtenOn": "2022-06-10T00:28:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not an expert on this code, but it is a common pattern to allocate arrays based on the max enum element. So if we a KMI interface that references:\n```\nstruct foo {\n  ...\n  int attr[NUM_NL80211_ATTR];\n  ...\n};\n```\nso enum size often does affect KMI. You may be right that it doesn\u0027t in this case (at least not now)...but who\u0027s to say such a dependency might not be added later? Or added in a vendor module where we can\u0027t see it? For that reason, our ABI checking tools do flag enum size changes if an enum is part of a KMI interface. \n\nThere is still the danger that someone will rely on a temporary definition so forcing folks to add a line like:\n```\n#define NL80211_ATTR_MAX_NUM_AKM_SUITES NL80211_ATTR_RESERVED_DO_NOT_USE_1\n```\nmakes it clear that they cannot rely on that value being stable. There is no doubt that they are doing something unsafe. If someone comes back to me and complains that a change in this value broke them, I\u0027d point to this line and say \"bummer for you, you shouldn\u0027t have done that\".\n\nAnyway, I\u0027m open to other proposals. Let me know when you are ready to have this series reviewed.",
